Dijkstra
(vertex, distanceFromSource)

Example:

(B, 100)

means:

Shortest known distance from A to B is 100.
Prim's
(vertex, edgeWeight)

Example:

(B, 40)

means:

I can connect B to my current tree
using an edge of weight 40.

Notice:

Nothing about distance from A.
What gets minimized?
Dijkstra
dist[v]

Distance from source to every vertex.

Final:

dist[A]=0
dist[C]=40
dist[D]=80
dist[B]=100

because:

A -> B = 100
A -> C -> D -> B = 120

Choose 100.

Prim's

Minimize:

sum of all edges in the tree

Choose:

A-C = 40
C-D = 40
D-B = 40

Tree cost:

40 + 40 + 40 = 120
Important observation
Distance from A to B inside MST
A -> C -> D -> B = 120

But shortest path is:

A -> B = 100

Prim's is completely okay with this!

Why?

Because Prim's says:

I don't care that A to B became 120.

I only care that the entire tree cost is minimum.

Dijkstra says:

I don't care about the total cost of connecting everyone.

I only care about the shortest path from A.
Update formula
Dijkstra
if (dist[u] + wt < dist[v]) {
    dist[v] = dist[u] + wt;
}

Because reaching v depends on:

distance to u + edge(u,v)
Prim's
if (wt < key[v]) {
    key[v] = wt;
}

Because connecting v only depends on:

the cheapest edge entering v
One sentence summary
Dijkstra	Prim's
Minimize distance from source to each vertex.	Minimize total cost of the spanning tree.
Path problem	Tree problem
Source-centric	Graph-centric
The mental model
Dijkstra:
"How do I travel from A to everyone cheaply?"

Prim's:
"How do I connect everyone cheaply?"

That one distinction is the entire difference between the two algorithms.
Exactly! 💯

A spanning tree has one job:

Connect all vertices using exactly V - 1 edges and without cycles.

Prim's is trying to minimize:

sum of all edges in the tree

while making sure:

every vertex is included exactly once in the connected tree.

In your example:

      100
   A ------- B
   |         |
  40        40
   |         |
   C ------- D
       40

If we choose the direct edge:

A-B = 100

we still need to connect C and D, so the tree costs:

100 + 40 + 40 = 180

Instead, if we avoid the expensive 100 edge:

A-C = 40
C-D = 40
D-B = 40

we get:

40 + 40 + 40 = 120

which is smaller.

The important realization is:

Shortest path from A to B = 100
Distance from A to B in MST = 120

and that's perfectly okay because the MST is not trying to minimize A → B.

It's trying to minimize:

Cost of connecting {A, B, C, D} together.

This is the sentence I use to remember it:

Dijkstra thinks locally about one source and all destinations.

Prim's thinks globally about connecting the entire graph as cheaply as possible.

