The fundamental difference
Dijkstra says:
I don't care about the cost of connecting everybody.
I only care about the cheapest way to reach each vertex from A.
Prim's says:
I don't care if some source-to-destination distances become larger.
I only care about the minimum total cost of the entire tree.

That's the exact philosophical difference between the two algorithms.

dijkstra  is about distance of each vertex from source ?

Yes, exactly. 🎯

Dijkstra's algorithm is fundamentally about:

Finding the shortest distance from one source vertex to every other vertex.

If the source is A, then it computes:

dist[A] = shortest distance from A to A
dist[B] = shortest distance from A to B
dist[C] = shortest distance from A to C
dist[D] = shortest distance from A to D
...

For example:

      100
   A ------- B
   |         |
  40        40
   |         |
   C ------- D
       40

Source = A

Then:

dist[A] = 0
dist[C] = 40
dist[D] = 80      (A -> C -> D)
dist[B] = 100     (direct edge)

because:

A -> C -> D -> B = 120

is worse than:

A -> B = 100
Think of dist[] as:
dist[v]

means:

"What is the cheapest possible cost to reach v starting from my source?"

That's why the relaxation is:
if (dist[u] + wt < dist[v]) {
    dist[v] = dist[u] + wt;
}

because to reach v, you first have to reach u, and then take the edge u → v.

One-line memory trick
Dijkstra  → shortest distance FROM ONE SOURCE to every vertex.

Prim's    → minimum cost TO CONNECT all vertices.

That's the exact distinction.